    "result_text": "All tests pass. Here's a summary of what I implemented.\n\n## Change\n\n`POST /api/owners` now records `namesakeCount` \u2014 the number of existing owners that already share the new owner's first and last name (case-insensitive) at creation time \u2014 and `GET /api/owners/{id}` returns it.\n\n## Files touched\n\n**New logic (each a small single-responsibility unit, mirroring the existing `Household`/`EnsureUniqueHousehold` pair):**\n- `Namesake.java` \u2014 the one definition of \"namesake\": case-insensitive first+last name match. Reusable membership test.\n- `CountNamesakes.java` \u2014 pipeline step that counts matching existing owners on the built owner and stores the count before `SaveOwner` persists it (so the new owner isn't counted among its own namesakes \u2014 same \"count before save\" pattern as `AssignOwnerCustomerCode`).\n\n**Wiring:**\n- `owners.POST.yml` \u2014 inserted a `countNamesakes` step (under `transaction` governance) between `assignCustomerCode` and `save`.\n\n**Model / persistence / contract:**\n- `Owner.java` \u2014 added `namesakeCount` field (`namesake_count` column) with getter/setter.\n- All four `schema.sql` files (hsqldb, h2, mysql, postgres) \u2014 added the `namesake_count` column.\n- `openapi.yml` \u2014 added the read-only `namesakeCount` property to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` carries it and MapStruct maps it by name automatically.\n\nThe `AcceptanceTest.coreCountsNamesakes` case (two owners sharing first+last, different postcodes) passes, asserting the second owner reads back `namesakeCount: 1`, and the full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`).",
